      "body": "I can reproduce this on the current Homarr **v1.72.0** Docker image on an ARM64 home server (Debian 12, 8 GB RAM). Nearly 2 GB for a dashboard is excessive and puts significant memory pressure on a small server, so I would really appreciate a fix.\n\nI have historical container metrics from Beszel covering the complete lifetime of the current container. After the container started, memory grew as follows (UTC):\n\n| Time | Container memory |\n|---|---:|\n| Jul 25 07:20 | 516 MB |\n| Jul 25 15:20 | 1,009 MB |\n| Jul 25 23:20 | 1,501 MB |\n| Jul 26 05:40 | 1,867 MB |\n| Jul 27 01:40 | 1,950 MB |\n| Jul 29 09:40 | 1,943 MB |\n\nThe growth was therefore roughly 516 MB -> 1.87 GB in about 22 hours. It then plateaued around 1.9 GB instead of returning near its initial level. A current 50-second sample stayed between 1.885 and 1.887 GiB at only 0.06-0.31% CPU.\n\nThis is almost entirely the `next-server` process, not Redis or Linux page cache:\n\n```text\nnext-server (v16.2.10)\nVmRSS:        1,947,240 kB\nRssAnon:      1,906,784 kB\nPrivateDirty: 1,814,228 kB\nVmSwap:          97,444 kB\nRedis:              ~1 MB\n```\n\nThe container cgroup reports about 2.08 GB current and 2.12 GB peak usage. It has not been OOM-killed and has not restarted, so this is the retained memory of one continuous four-day run. There were no heap/OOM/ENOMEM messages in the last 24 hours of logs.\n\nThe process does release small amounts intermittently, but the baseline remains around 1.9 GB. This looks like substantial persistent retention/leak rather than memory needed only during a short request. Please let me know if a heap snapshot or any other diagnostic from this instance would help.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
